ket cap  and the realized market cap total market  cap simply takes the number of  outstanding bitcoin times the price per  bitcoin that's what you see in that 1.16  trillion dollar market cap but what the  realized cap is it looks at when was the  last time that this bitcoin actually  traded hands and what was the price at  that moment so if i last sold a bitcoin  let's say to joseph at one thousand  dollars that would count as one thousand  dollars towards the realized cap versus  the 62 000 that it would count towards  the market cap and so when you look at  the difference between the total market  cap and the realized market cap and then  you divide it by the standard deviation  of the market cap you can score it what  we know is that when we cross over three  it usually leads to a drastic increase  in price and we just reclaimed that  three there and so when we see these  types of movements there usually ends up  being a market structure that has a very  bullish story behind it but last but not  least dylan leclair coming out and  showing that the long-term holders are  still in accumulation mode even though  85 of bitcoin has not moved in the last  90 days long-term holders are still  buying more and he said that typically  we see these during bear markets or  consolidation periods but instead we are  watching this happen as bitcoin's price  has doubled over the last couple of  months from 30 000 now up over 60 000  so to see those long-term holders  continuing to accumulate into price  strength like this is very very bullish  and so when we start to look at what is  actually occurring in the market and  because of the on-chain metrics and that  open transparent ledger that bitcoin  provides we can see that not only has  bitcoin been very strong over the last  10 years over the last year but also the  market structure and the setup is very  interesting now on top of that we've got  the bitcoin etf coming we're going to  talk about that later in the show but  a